00:44:15.285 --> 00:44:17.821
They all love to wrestle and
they're really good at it.

00:44:17.821 --> 00:44:22.826 align:start size:97% position:3%
And he recognizes that it
would be neither fair nor safe

00:44:24.628 --> 00:44:29.299
for his little girls to
go and compete on a mat

00:44:29.299 --> 00:44:32.069
against the biological boy.

00:44:32.069 --> 00:44:34.672
There are physiological
differences that exist

00:44:34.672 --> 00:44:36.740 align:start size:84% position:16%
between boys and girls
and men and women.

00:44:36.740 --> 00:44:39.009
And when you look to, and
this is what our bill does,

00:44:39.009 --> 00:44:42.079
it clarifies upholds and
strengthens title nine

00:44:42.079 --> 00:44:44.481
which was enacted in 1972,

00:44:44.481 --> 00:44:47.651
out of that recognition
that there are biological

00:44:47.651 --> 00:44:51.055 align:start size:97% position:3%
and physiological distinctions
between boys and girls,

00:44:51.055 --> 00:44:52.289
men and women.

00:44:52.289 --> 00:44:56.593
And title nine ensures
that girls and women

00:44:56.593 --> 00:45:00.297 align:start size:94% position:6%
are able to play in sports
and compete in sports

00:45:00.297 --> 00:45:02.366
on a level playing field.

00:45:03.801 --> 00:45:07.104 align:start size:97% position:3%
Title nine is being undermined
and weakened in some areas,

00:45:07.104 --> 00:45:11.275 align:start size:94% position:6%
as States are
interpreting what it does

00:45:11.275 --> 00:45:12.776
in very different ways.

00:45:12.776 --> 00:45:15.479 align:start size:97% position:3%
And as a result, as we've seen
in places like Connecticut,

00:45:15.479 --> 00:45:16.880
you have girls who are,

00:45:16.880 --> 00:45:18.182 align:start size:94% position:6%
they're about to graduate
from high school.

00:45:18.182 --> 00:45:20.284 align:start size:88% position:13%
They're vying for
scholarships to college

00:45:20.284 --> 00:45:24.955 align:start size:84% position:16%
and losing in track
and field competitions

00:45:24.955 --> 00:45:28.859
because they have all of
a sudden now been faced

00:45:28.859 --> 00:45:32.996
with a competitor who has
a very direct physiological

00:45:32.996 --> 00:45:36.333
advantage as a biological male.

00:45:36.333 --> 00:45:38.168 align:start size:88% position:13%
And they're seeing
their scholarship hopes

00:45:38.168 --> 00:45:41.205 align:start size:84% position:16%
and dreams be taken
away from them.

00:45:41.205 --> 00:45:42.473
And so this really just says,

00:45:42.473 --> 00:45:45.109
hey, this is about making sure

00:45:45.109 --> 00:45:49.213 align:start position:0%
that all of those opportunities
that were provided to women

00:45:49.213 --> 00:45:51.815 align:start size:88% position:13%
and girls, because
title nine was in place,

00:45:51.815 --> 00:45:55.719
are allowed to continue for
this generation and the next.

00:45:55.719 --> 00:45:57.621 align:start size:88% position:13%
It doesn't discriminate
against anyone.

00:45:57.621 --> 00:45:59.623 align:start size:94% position:6%
It just simply says, if
you're a biological female

00:45:59.623 --> 00:46:00.858
you shouldn't be
forced to compete

00:46:00.858 --> 00:46:02.826 align:start size:84% position:16%
in sports against
the biological male.

00:46:02.826 --> 00:46:04.828
- Do you want to
make any comments

00:46:04.828 --> 00:46:08.165
about trans people before
the haters get in here?

00:46:08.165 --> 00:46:10.467
Because I know you have no
problem with trans people

00:46:10.467 --> 00:46:12.770
but I know that they'll
hear what you just said

00:46:12.770 --> 00:46:14.304
and say she's a transphobe.

00:46:14.304 --> 00:46:16.874
So do you want to just
just clean it up for them

00:46:16.874 --> 00:46:18.509
before they get to it?

00:46:18.509 --> 00:46:23.514
- I think that people are
allowed and free to be able

00:46:24.948 --> 00:46:27.684
to make their own choices
and live in this country

00:46:27.684 --> 00:46:31.922 align:start size:97% position:3%
as Americans and be treated
justly under the laws

00:46:31.922 --> 00:46:33.157
as we all are.

00:46:34.825 --> 00:46:37.060
If you again, look
at my legislation

00:46:37.060 --> 00:46:39.763
you'll recognize that
this very simply points

00:46:39.763 --> 00:46:43.467 align:start size:94% position:6%
to biological differences
between men and women

00:46:43.467 --> 00:46:48.071 align:start position:0%
and upholds the incredible
game-changing and life-changing

00:46:48.071 --> 00:46:50.607 align:start size:88% position:13%
opportunities that
title nine created back

00:46:50.607 --> 00:46:52.075
in the early seventies.

00:46:52.075 --> 00:46:53.844
- All right so the next
one that you went for

00:46:53.844 --> 00:46:55.746
because you really just
pulled out all the stops

00:46:55.746 --> 00:46:58.315
on your way out the door.

00:46:58.315 --> 00:47:01.518
You tried to pass
an abortion bill

00:47:01.518 --> 00:47:02.986
and we have a similar,

00:47:02.986 --> 00:47:04.121 align:start size:97% position:3%
we talked about abortion last
time you were on the show

00:47:04.121 --> 00:47:06.190 align:start size:84% position:16%
and we have a
similar stance on it.

00:47:06.190 --> 00:47:10.561 align:start size:94% position:6%
My position before I wrote
my book was 20 weeks.

00:47:10.561 --> 00:47:13.697
Because at that point, we know
that the fetus can feel pain.

00:47:13.697 --> 00:47:15.032 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now there's actually
a little evidence

00:47:15.032 --> 00:47:16.867
that it's closer to 18 weeks.

00:47:16.867 --> 00:47:19.770
But then in my book, I took
the first trimester position

00:47:19.770 --> 00:47:22.039
which is about 12
weeks in effect.

00:47:22.039 --> 00:47:25.409
And I talk about, I concede
that I believe it's a life.

00:47:25.409 --> 00:47:27.578
I tried to talk about
as many of the moral

00:47:27.578 --> 00:47:29.513 align:start size:84% position:16%
and philosophical
and religious issues

00:47:29.513 --> 00:47:30.747
and all of those things.

00:47:30.747 --> 00:47:32.015
I think, you know
that David and I

00:47:32.015 --> 00:47:34.051
are in the midst of
surrogacy right now.

00:47:34.051 --> 00:47:37.487 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I'm like well aware
of all of the stuff

00:47:37.487 --> 00:47:38.655
related to this, right?

00:47:39.890 --> 00:47:42.993
You put forth a bill
that, as I said earlier

00:47:42.993 --> 00:47:46.029 align:start size:88% position:13%
was the democratic
position for pretty much

00:47:46.029 --> 00:47:48.532
the last three decades or so,

00:47:48.532 --> 00:47:50.701 align:start size:94% position:6%
but is not the democratic
position anymore.

00:47:50.701 --> 00:47:52.135
So can you lay it out for us?

00:47:53.670 --> 00:47:55.739
- Sure it is,

00:47:55.739 --> 00:47:57.307
I introduced two bills.

00:47:57.307 --> 00:48:01.745 align:start size:84% position:16%
One is the Late Term
Abortion Ban Act.

00:48:01.745 --> 00:48:06.750 align:start position:0%
And the other is the Born
Alive Survivors Protection Act.

00:48:09.887 --> 00:48:12.489
There were similar
pieces of legislation

00:48:12.489 --> 00:48:15.325
that were introduced
previously in Congress,

00:48:15.325 --> 00:48:19.930
that had some minor
issues with the bill

00:48:19.930 --> 00:48:21.765 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I felt that I needed
to vote against it.

00:48:21.765 --> 00:48:24.101
For example, the Late
Term Abortion Act,

00:48:24.101 --> 00:48:25.569
there had been a similar bill

00:48:25.569 --> 00:48:30.040 align:start size:97% position:3%
that was introduced previously
that provided an exception

00:48:31.475 --> 00:48:34.645
to the bill if the life of
the mother was at stake,

00:48:34.645 --> 00:48:37.681 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it did not allow
for any exception

00:48:37.681 --> 00:48:42.219 align:start size:84% position:16%
if there were severe
and irreversible

00:48:42.219 --> 00:48:45.989 align:start position:0%
health and physical consequences
at stake for the mother.

00:48:45.989 --> 00:48:47.591
So the bill that I introduced,

00:48:47.591 --> 00:48:49.793
so I felt that I had to
oppose it at that time

00:48:49.793 --> 00:48:51.695 align:start size:88% position:13%
because it did not
provide that exception.

00:48:51.695 --> 00:48:54.698
The bill that I introduced
included that essentially,

00:48:57.301 --> 00:49:01.772 align:start size:88% position:13%
but was mostly the
same as the other bill.

00:49:01.772 --> 00:49:06.777
And the born alive for
babies who are born alive

00:49:07.878 --> 00:49:08.879 align:start size:84% position:16%
even after an
attempted abortion,

00:49:10.280 --> 00:49:12.749 align:start size:97% position:3%
there are laws in place
that should be strengthened

00:49:12.749 --> 00:49:16.787 align:start size:97% position:3%
to make sure that any doctor
and healthcare provider

00:49:16.787 --> 00:49:20.557
in that situation must
do all that they can

00:49:20.557 --> 00:49:23.093
to save that baby's life,

00:49:23.093 --> 00:49:26.330
no matter how that baby
got to that situation.

00:49:26.330 --> 00:49:29.599 align:start size:88% position:13%
Just as they would be
required to do under law

00:49:29.599 --> 00:49:32.569
for a baby that was
born prematurely,

00:49:32.569 --> 00:49:35.205
maybe at that same age.

00:49:37.007 --> 00:49:41.478
To me these are bills
that every American

00:49:41.478 --> 00:49:43.613
frankly should support.

00:49:44.781 --> 00:49:46.683
- Right, so I could
get not supporting it

00:49:46.683 --> 00:49:48.852 align:start size:84% position:16%
if you were completely
against abortion

00:49:48.852 --> 00:49:50.854
because you just wouldn't
want those bills to exist.

00:49:50.854 --> 00:49:53.390 align:start size:84% position:16%
But for the group
of people that have,

00:49:53.390 --> 00:49:55.692
some gray area there,

00:49:55.692 --> 00:49:58.328 align:start size:84% position:16%
what's your actual
position on the cutoff

00:49:58.328 --> 00:50:00.931 align:start size:94% position:6%
of your first trimester
also, if I'm not mistaken?

00:50:02.065 --> 00:50:03.300
- The bill is 20 weeks.

00:50:03.300 --> 00:50:05.936 align:start size:84% position:16%
It is modeled after,
as you mentioned,

00:50:05.936 --> 00:50:08.605 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's been a lot of
science that's based around

00:50:10.240 --> 00:50:13.744
when the baby can
start to feel pain.

00:50:13.744 --> 00:50:16.646
And that's what the
bill is based on.

00:50:16.646 --> 00:50:19.716
- Yeah do you find
that even on abortion

00:50:19.716 --> 00:50:23.387
that it's easier in an odd
way to talk to Republicans?

00:50:23.387 --> 00:50:26.323 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think the average Republican
or conservative let's say

00:50:26.323 --> 00:50:27.924
is gonna disagree
with you on this.

00:50:27.924 --> 00:50:29.726 align:start size:88% position:13%
They absolutely disagree
with me on this.

00:50:29.726 --> 00:50:31.228
The amount of letters
that I got saying,

00:50:31.228 --> 00:50:32.362
Dave I loved your book.

00:50:32.362 --> 00:50:34.031 align:start size:88% position:13%
You're wrong on
abortion, but it's okay.

00:50:34.031 --> 00:50:36.533
But we can agree to disagree.

00:50:36.533 --> 00:50:39.903 align:start size:97% position:3%
Do you find that even people
who completely disagree

00:50:39.903 --> 00:50:41.972
with you on this, on the right

00:50:41.972 --> 00:50:43.673
are still willing to
talk to you about it?

00:50:43.673 --> 00:50:47.544 align:start size:94% position:6%
Where to take that 20-week
position on the left,

00:50:47.544 --> 00:50:49.546 align:start size:84% position:16%
is pretty much
impossible these days.

00:50:50.981 --> 00:50:52.549
- Yeah which is unfortunate.

00:50:52.549 --> 00:50:57.020
I mean, for me personally,
I am personally pro-life.

00:50:57.020 --> 00:51:01.124 align:start size:97% position:3%
I would never make that
choice to have an abortion.

00:51:02.659 --> 00:51:06.296 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's often one of the
most difficult decisions

00:51:06.296 --> 00:51:10.100
that any woman may
make in her life.

00:51:10.100 --> 00:51:13.203
And it really comes down to,

00:51:13.203 --> 00:51:17.507 align:start size:97% position:3%
what role does the government
have to play into this?

00:51:17.507 --> 00:51:20.644 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so this is a much,
much bigger topic

00:51:20.644 --> 00:51:25.315
that we as Americans should
come together and talk about

00:51:25.315 --> 00:51:28.752 align:start size:88% position:13%
and discuss because
when you implement laws,

00:51:28.752 --> 00:51:31.221
there are consequences that come

00:51:31.221 --> 00:51:33.790 line:0%
with those laws and
there's a country,

00:51:33.790 --> 00:51:36.893 line:0%
I believe it might've, I
think it was Argentina.

00:51:36.893 --> 00:51:38.829 line:0%
Might be mistaken, it was
a South American country.

00:51:38.829 --> 00:51:40.797 align:start size:84% position:16%
That for example,
had very strict laws

00:51:40.797 --> 00:51:45.802
in place where they ended
up actually arresting women

00:51:47.270 --> 00:51:50.273
who had had miscarriages
because it was a violation

00:51:50.273 --> 00:51:52.776
of their anti-abortion laws.

00:51:52.776 --> 00:51:57.747 align:start size:84% position:16%
So we've gotta go
through this question,

00:51:59.182 --> 00:52:00.617
morally as a society and the
decision that people are making

00:52:00.617 --> 00:52:04.688 align:start size:97% position:3%
for themselves and the
impacts of that personally,

00:52:04.688 --> 00:52:07.457
but also what role government

00:52:07.457 --> 00:52:12.462 align:start size:97% position:3%
should have in this
incredibly personal decision

00:52:13.897 --> 00:52:18.168
that a woman may make for
herself and her family.

00:52:19.336 --> 00:52:23.306
So I've seen just going
back to your question,

00:52:24.407 --> 00:52:26.042 align:start size:84% position:16%
yeah, I mean,
there are Democrats

00:52:26.042 --> 00:52:28.545
and people who consider
themselves progressives

00:52:28.545 --> 00:52:32.749 align:start size:97% position:3%
who really believe that there
should be no restrictions

00:52:32.749 --> 00:52:34.618
whatsoever on abortion.

00:52:34.618 --> 00:52:36.086
I completely disagree with that.

00:52:36.086 --> 00:52:39.656 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think that to a
certain point women need

00:52:39.656 --> 00:52:43.059
to be able to make this
decision for themselves,

00:52:43.059 --> 00:52:46.763 align:start size:97% position:3%
but there should be very
clear restrictions in place.

00:52:46.763 --> 00:52:49.266 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that we as a society,
should come together

00:52:49.266 --> 00:52:54.271
to find ways to make it so
that this is a rare decision.

00:52:55.105 --> 00:52:56.373
That is put in place.

00:52:56.373 --> 00:52:59.075
And unfortunately,
that's not something

00:52:59.075 --> 00:53:03.813 align:start size:88% position:13%
that is being pushed,
especially by Democrats.

00:53:03.813 --> 00:53:06.283 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, and that's so
unfortunate because it's like,

00:53:06.283 --> 00:53:08.185
man, if you guys would
just show us a little

00:53:08.185 --> 00:53:10.153
of that gray area, you
could still keep people.

00:53:10.153 --> 00:53:13.356 align:start position:0%
But I always, I've debated
abortion with plenty of pro-life

00:53:13.356 --> 00:53:15.792
people on my show, Ben
Shapiro and Dennis Prager

00:53:15.792 --> 00:53:16.993
and a whole bunch of others.

00:53:16.993 --> 00:53:18.261
And I always say
to them at the end,

00:53:18.261 --> 00:53:20.330
you know you guys make
compelling arguments,

00:53:20.330 --> 00:53:23.533
but in a weird way it's
the craziness of the left

00:53:23.533 --> 00:53:26.770 align:start size:88% position:13%
that has made me
slightly more pro-life.

00:53:26.770 --> 00:53:28.572
Because if you're gonna tell
me, you can have an abortion

00:53:28.572 --> 00:53:32.209
at eight months, I
just can't get there.

00:53:32.209 --> 00:53:33.677
- No, I agree.

00:53:33.677 --> 00:53:37.013 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, let's do a little
bit of a future stuff

00:53:37.013 --> 00:53:38.815
before we let you go.

00:53:38.815 --> 00:53:40.684
So first we'll do some
quick political stuff

00:53:40.684 --> 00:53:42.552 align:start size:84% position:16%
then I want to do
some personal stuff.

00:53:42.552 --> 00:53:43.987
So, all right.

00:53:43.987 --> 00:53:46.489 align:start size:97% position:3%
It does look like Joe Biden
will be the president,

00:53:46.489 --> 00:53:49.159
Kamala Harris will
be vice president.

00:53:49.159 --> 00:53:51.728 align:start position:0%
Do you sense despite everything
we've talked about here

00:53:51.728 --> 00:53:53.763 align:start size:94% position:6%
and obviously what's
unfolding in DC right now,

00:53:53.763 --> 00:53:57.434
that this idea of healing,
I know you want it to happen

00:53:57.434 --> 00:53:59.669 align:start size:97% position:3%
but you know, we're being
told now it's the time to heal

00:53:59.669 --> 00:54:01.171
and all of this stuff.

00:54:01.171 --> 00:54:02.672 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you sense that we're
really going to get there?

00:54:02.672 --> 00:54:05.208
Or is it just kinda,

00:54:05.208 --> 00:54:08.545
a little bit of lip
service for now?

00:54:08.545 --> 00:54:12.549
- I think the jury is
still out right now.

00:54:12.549 --> 00:54:17.554
Again, I very much hope
that we see a major shift

00:54:18.989 --> 00:54:22.692
in our leadership, and yes the
tone that the president sets

00:54:22.692 --> 00:54:25.495
is very important, but
it's gotta be bigger

00:54:25.495 --> 00:54:27.530
and more far reaching than that.

00:54:27.530 --> 00:54:30.800 align:start size:81% position:19%
So, we'll see what
happens there.

00:54:33.270 --> 00:54:36.239 align:start size:97% position:3%
We'll see what happens through
the nomination process,

00:54:36.239 --> 00:54:38.508
the kinds of people
that we've seen,

00:54:39.909 --> 00:54:43.613 align:start size:97% position:3%
Biden choose as his nominees
to serve in his cabinet.

00:54:44.881 --> 00:54:47.484
To me, some of the
problems that I see

00:54:47.484 --> 00:54:52.022
very directly are that
many of them represent

00:54:52.022 --> 00:54:55.158
the same kind of corruption,

00:54:55.158 --> 00:54:57.060
legal corruption in Washington,

00:54:57.060 --> 00:54:58.662
that we've seen for a long time.

00:54:58.662 --> 00:55:00.664
Where you have people who rotate

00:55:00.664 --> 00:55:03.333
through this revolving door
of working in government,

00:55:03.333 --> 00:55:04.801
then you go work as a lobbyist,

00:55:04.801 --> 00:55:06.670 align:start size:94% position:6%
then you go get money
from a defense contractor,

00:55:06.670 --> 00:55:08.872
then you go back in and work
in the department of defense.

00:55:08.872 --> 00:55:12.409
And this is what we see in
who he's nominated to serve

00:55:12.409 --> 00:55:13.610
as secretary of defense.

00:55:13.610 --> 00:55:16.379 align:start size:84% position:16%
He was yanked off
the board of Raytheon

00:55:16.379 --> 00:55:19.849 align:start size:97% position:3%
to go and now be the nominee
for secretary of defense.

00:55:19.849 --> 00:55:23.053 align:start size:97% position:3%
You see this with the
secretary of state nominee,

00:55:23.053 --> 00:55:26.022
Tony Blinken, who's just
made a whole bunch of money

00:55:26.022 --> 00:55:29.926 align:start size:88% position:13%
from defense contractors
in the time between

00:55:29.926 --> 00:55:34.397
the Biden administration, the
Obama administration and now.

00:55:34.397 --> 00:55:37.233 align:start size:97% position:3%
What to speak of the policies
that a lot of these folks

00:55:37.233 --> 00:55:40.303 align:start size:94% position:6%
represent which are really
centered around looking

00:55:40.303 --> 00:55:44.441 align:start size:88% position:13%
at their track records,
continuing these
costly warmongering

00:55:44.441 --> 00:55:48.011 align:start size:97% position:3%
regime change interventions
that have proven

00:55:48.011 --> 00:55:51.147 align:start size:88% position:13%
to be incredibly
destructive and costly.

00:55:51.147 --> 00:55:52.782
Both for us here
in this country,

00:55:52.782 --> 00:55:55.585
our troops, our people,
as well as the people

00:55:55.585 --> 00:55:58.621 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the countries where
these Wars have been waged.

